      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF), we can list out the factors of each number.\nThe factors of 40 are 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, and 40.\nThe factors of 48 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, and 48.\nThe common factors of 40 and 48 are 1, 2, 4, 8.\nThe greatest common factor is 8.\nTherefore, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\boxed{8}.\n\nProblem 5:\n\nWhat is the value of \\(\\tan \\pi + \\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) ?\nTo determine the value of \\(\\tan \\pi + \\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\), we need to calculate each term separately and then sum them up.\n\nFirst, let's evaluate \\(\\tan \\pi\\):\nThe tangent function, \\(\\tan x\\), is defined as the ratio of \\(\\sin x\\) to \\(\\cos x\\). For any angle \\(x\\), \\(\\tan x = 0\\) if and only if \\(\\sin x = 0\\) and \\(\\cos x \\neq 0\\). The value of \\(\\sin \\pi\\) is 0 and the value of \\(\\cos \\pi\\) is -1, so:\n\\[\n\\tan \\pi = \\frac{\\sin \\pi}{\\cos \\pi} = \\frac{0}{-1} = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, let's evaluate \\(\\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\):\nThe cotangent function is the reciprocal of the tangent function, \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\). Thus,\n\\[\n\\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{1}{\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, let's evaluate \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\):\nThe sine function, \\(\\sin x\\), is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the hypotenuse in a right triangle. For the angle \\(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) (which is 30 degrees), we have a 30-60-90 triangle where the length of the side opposite the 30-degree angle is 1/2 of the hypotenuse. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we sum these values:\n\\[\n\\tan \\pi + \\cot \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6} = 0 + 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]\n\nIn how many different ways can 5 students stand in a row for a photo if - The Solution Splash\nQuestion: In how many different ways can 5 students stand in a row for a photo if - Let\\pr(2,2)\nThese are the combinations of the groups#. The two loops around each group# will be points of rotation in the arrangement. Note that all arrangements are symmetric with respect to the two loops around the group#. Call the 5 students A, B, C, D, E. By symmetry,some sequence\\,#s will count as the same group#. Case #1. Students are in the order ABCDE, ABECD, CDEAB, BCDEA, BDC, DABC. Then, the # of different ways is\\example. These could begin with AD, BAC, CBAD. There are at least 8 symmetric groups. +\nDCCED  in six different orders: what are A and B sitting in? 9: Rotations are 0, 90°, 180°, 270°, 360°, 450°, 540°, 630°, and rearange only A, B and C index. Abwe. These .\n\n1 point\n +, - , , , .      D\n, F             H              NR    AB\n    , - ,D     AB      , , , , , ,          AÐ\n      , 0 , , 4   , BA      , , , , ,          Emb   C\n   , Å      &     , , , , , , ,      & B        C   C\n|          F      J         DABF     , &C      AÑ\n|                      J        \"B    &     CE\n\\                      K    \"ABF     B\n\\                    BD\n\\\n\\\n1, , , , 15\nFRA\nNA), BNA) K\nBTAA),FTA\nBNA,\n\nA",
