      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of two numbers, we can use the method of listing their factors. This process involves writing down all the factors of both numbers and identifying the largest one that appears in both lists.\n\nLet's start with the given numbers: 40 and 48.\n\n1. **Factors of 40:**\n   - 40 can be divided evenly by numbers ranging from 1 up to 40.\n   - Following this, we list the factors:\n     \\[\n     1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 40\n     \\]\n\n2. **Factors of 48:**\n   - Similarly, 48 can be divided evenly by numbers ranging from 1 up to 48.\n   - Following this, we list the factors:\n     \\[\n     1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 48\n     \\]\n\n3. **Identifying Common Factors:**\n   - Now, we look for the numbers that appear in both lists.\n   - The common factors of 40 and 48 are:\n     \\[\n     1, 2, 4, 8\n     \\]\n\n4. **Determining the Greatest Common Factor:**\n   - From the list of common factors, the greatest number is 8.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48 is \\boxed{8}.",
